# Provider-event-bus no-self-trigger rule

When a React provider, context, or hook both **dispatches** and **listens** to the
same `CustomEvent` (or any pub-sub signal), either:

  (a) a self-vs-external flag exists in `event.detail` AND the listener ignores
      self-origin, OR
  (b) the dispatch site is elsewhere — not inside the listener's effective
      subscription closure.

The choice MUST be explicit in the implementation. "Both happen to work" is a
bug-shaped coincidence.

Any effect whose deps include state that the same provider updates is suspect —
verify the effect's re-fire path cannot re-enter its own state setter.

## Smoke test

Any provider that fires a network request on event MUST be tested with exactly
one fetch per mount:

```ts
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { test } from 'node:test';
// ... stub fetch, render provider, assert callCount === 1
```

## Worked counterexample — D58, 2026-09-18

`apps/web/components/chrome/sign-in-context.tsx`'s `refresh()` dispatched a
`corpus:auth-changed` CustomEvent after every `setMe/setMeState`. The provider
also had a `useEffect` subscribed to that same event that called `refresh()`
again. Provider mount → mount-time `useEffect` → `refresh()` → dispatch →
listener catches own dispatch → `refresh()` → dispatch → infinite. The bug
shipped to PR #185 because no smoke test asserted `/me` fires exactly once per
mount. Fix shape: dispatch removed from inside `refresh()`; the self-firing
listener removed; a `mountedRef` guard added at the mount-time effect to handle
`reactStrictMode: true`'s double-invoke. See `sign-in-context.tsx` lines
240-275 for the canonical pattern.

## Worked counterexample — D58 Phase 2

Even after the loop was removed, `/me` fired twice on first hydration under
`reactStrictMode: true` because the mount-time effect ran twice in dev (probe
+ real mount) before either commit landed. The `mountedRef` pattern
(set-after-check, never reset) is the canonical React-StrictMode-safe mount
guard. See `sign-in-context.tsx` for the live implementation.

